---
import PhaseLayout from '../../layouts/PhaseLayout.astro';
---

<PhaseLayout title="The Polymath Mind — Cross-Domain Mastery Program" activeNav="courses">

  <div class="nav" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:6px;">
    <button class="tn active" onclick="show(0)">The Polymath Framework</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(1)">Mathematics</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(2)">Physics</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(3)">Computer Science</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(4)">Engineering & Robotics</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(5)">Software Engineering</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(6)">AI/ML/DL Integration</button>
    <button class="tn" onclick="show(7)">The Synthesis Layer</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================
       TAB 0 — THE POLYMATH FRAMEWORK
       ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel active" id="p0">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-ai">Philosophy & Methodology</div>
      <div class="track-title">The Polymath Framework — how to master multiple domains</div>
      <div class="track-sub">The modern world rewards specialists. But the greatest breakthroughs — from da Vinci's anatomical art to Shannon's information theory to Musk's engineering empires — come from people who refused to stay in one lane. This framework is the operating system for building genuine cross-domain expertise, not shallow breadth.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm0">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm0')">
        <div class="step-num num-ai">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Mental Model: First Principles Thinking</div><div class="step-meta">Feynman / Musk / Aristotle</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm0">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm0">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">What it is</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Decompose any problem into its most fundamental truths — the axioms that cannot be reduced further — and reason up from there. Aristotle called it "the first basis from which a thing is known." Feynman used it to rebuild quantum electrodynamics. Musk used it to price-engineer rockets and batteries from raw materials instead of accepting industry benchmarks. First principles thinking is the universal solvent for crossing domain boundaries: when you strip away jargon, every field reduces to a small set of core truths that connect to other fields.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">How to practice it</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Pick any concept you are studying. Ask: "What are the 3-5 fundamental assumptions this rests on?" Then ask: "Which of these assumptions also appear in another domain I know?" Example: gradient descent in ML rests on (1) a differentiable loss surface, (2) iterative local search, (3) step-size control. These same three ideas appear in control systems (PID tuning), economics (price adjustment), and evolution (mutation rate). When you see the shared skeleton, you transfer intuition instantly.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://fs.blog/first-principles/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Farnam Street — First Principles: The Building Blocks of True Knowledge <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The best free essay on first principles thinking. Shane Parrish traces the concept from Aristotle through Feynman to modern applications. Read this before anything else in this program.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Read first</span><span class="rtag book">Essay</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NV3sBlRgzTI" target="_blank" class="res-link">Elon Musk explains First Principles — TED Talk <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The clearest public explanation of how Musk applied first principles to SpaceX rocket costs, Tesla battery pricing, and Boring Company tunneling. Watch for the method, not the celebrity.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Practice exercise:</b> Take any technology you use daily (a database, a neural network, a compiler). Write down its 5 fundamental assumptions. Then find one assumption that is shared with a completely different field. This 15-minute exercise rewires how you see knowledge — from vertical silos to a horizontal web.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm1">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm1')">
        <div class="step-num num-ai">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Mental Model: Latticework of Mental Models</div><div class="step-meta">Charlie Munger's worldly wisdom</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm1">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm1">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">The core idea</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Charlie Munger — Warren Buffett's partner and one of the most successful investors in history — argues that you need roughly 80-100 mental models drawn from all major disciplines (physics, biology, psychology, mathematics, economics, engineering, history) to make reliably good decisions. No single model is sufficient. "To the man with only a hammer, every problem looks like a nail." The latticework is the antidote: a mesh of models that lets you see the same problem from multiple angles simultaneously.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Key models to acquire from each domain</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Physics: entropy, feedback loops, critical mass, equilibrium. Biology: evolution, ecosystem dynamics, niche specialization, immune response. Mathematics: compounding, inversion, Bayesian updating, power laws. Psychology: incentive structures, confirmation bias, loss aversion, social proof. Engineering: redundancy, margin of safety, bottleneck analysis, fail-safes. Economics: opportunity cost, supply/demand, game theory, comparative advantage. Each model is a lens. Collect enough lenses and you develop compound vision.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://fs.blog/mental-models/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Farnam Street — Mental Models: The Best Way to Make Intelligent Decisions <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Comprehensive free catalog of ~100 mental models organized by discipline. Bookmark this page. Review one new model per week alongside your domain studies.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Reference catalog</span><span class="rtag book">Web</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Poor Charlie's Almanack" by Charlie Munger</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The definitive collection of Munger's speeches and essays on worldly wisdom and mental models. His 1994 speech "A Lesson on Elementary, Worldly Wisdom" is the foundational text for the latticework approach. Available in libraries and used bookstores.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Essential reading</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>The polymath advantage:</b> Most people learn models from one field and force-fit them everywhere. A software engineer sees everything as a systems problem. An economist sees everything as incentives. The polymath holds 10+ lenses simultaneously and picks the one that fits best. This is Munger's "worldly wisdom" — and it is the single strongest argument for the program you are about to undertake.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm2">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm2')">
        <div class="step-num num-ai">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">The Expertise Progression: T-shaped to Pi-shaped to Comb-shaped</div><div class="step-meta">Building your knowledge architecture</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm2">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm2">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">T</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Stage 1: T-shaped (Year 1-2)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">One deep vertical of expertise (your "spike") plus broad surface-level knowledge across many domains. You start here. Pick your first deep vertical — the one that pays your bills or fuels your obsession. Go deep enough to be in the top 10% of practitioners. For this program, that spike might be AI/ML, mathematics, or software engineering. The horizontal bar is your broad survey of all the other domains in this program.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#928;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Stage 2: Pi-shaped (Year 2-3)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Two deep verticals connected by broad knowledge. Now you have two spikes. The magic happens at the intersection — the problems only someone with BOTH deep skills can see. A mathematician who deeply understands ML can do things neither a pure mathematician nor a pure ML engineer can do alone. This is where you become genuinely rare and valuable.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9610;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Stage 3: Comb-shaped (Year 3-4+)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Multiple deep verticals — 3, 4, 5 domains of genuine depth — all connected by a thick horizontal bar of cross-domain knowledge. This is the polymath endstate. You can work at the intersection of physics, mathematics, computer science, and AI simultaneously. You see connections invisible to specialists. You become the person who can bridge any two teams, translate any two fields, and invent at any intersection. This is the goal of this entire program.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Your immediate action:</b> Decide your first spike. Which domain in this program will you take to genuine depth first? Commit to it for 6-12 months before adding a second spike. Breadth without one initial depth produces a dilettante, not a polymath. The order matters: depth first, then breadth, then more depth in a second domain.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm3">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm3')">
        <div class="step-num num-ai">4</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">How Polymaths Actually Learn — the meta-skill</div><div class="step-meta">Study strategies from history's greatest cross-domain thinkers</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm3">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm3">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">The Feynman Technique</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Richard Feynman could learn any subject by: (1) Studying the material. (2) Explaining it in simple language as if teaching a child. (3) Identifying where the explanation breaks down — those are your real gaps. (4) Going back and filling the gaps, then re-explaining. This forces deep understanding rather than surface familiarity. Apply it every single week to whatever you are studying.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Interleaving over blocking</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Cognitive science shows that mixing different subjects in a single study session (interleaving) produces deeper learning than studying one subject for hours (blocking). Study math for 45 minutes, then physics for 45 minutes, then CS for 45 minutes. The switching cost is real but the cross-pollination it produces is worth far more. Your brain finds connections that blocked study never reveals.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">The Spacing Effect + Active Recall</div>
              <div class="res-desc">You forget 80% of what you learn within 48 hours unless you actively retrieve it at increasing intervals. This is the spacing effect, the most replicated finding in cognitive science. Use Anki flashcards for every domain. 15 minutes of spaced retrieval per day retains more than 3 hours of passive re-reading per week. Create cards that force you to EXPLAIN, not just recognize.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Analogical reasoning — the polymath superpower</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The single most powerful cognitive tool for cross-domain work is reasoning by analogy. When learning a new concept, immediately ask: "What does this remind me of in a domain I already know?" Electrical circuits map to fluid dynamics. Neural networks map to biological neurons (loosely). Category theory maps to type systems. Every analogy you find is a bridge that lets you transfer years of intuition from one domain to another in minutes.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.amazon.com/Range-Generalists-Triumph-Specialized-World/dp/0735214484" target="_blank" class="res-link">"Range: Why Generalists Triumph in a Specialized World" by David Epstein <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The definitive modern case for polymathic thinking. Epstein dismantles the "10,000 hour rule" myth and shows how the most impactful innovators sample widely before specializing deeply. Full of research and case studies. Essential reading for anyone on this path.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Essential</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.amazon.com/Ultralearning-Master-Outsmart-Competition-Accelerate/dp/006285268X" target="_blank" class="res-link">"Ultralearning" by Scott Young <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Young taught himself the entire MIT CS curriculum in one year. This book is his methodology: metalearning, focus, directness, drilling, retrieval, experimentation, and feedback. Apply his 9 principles to every domain in this program.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Methodology</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>The non-obvious truth about polymaths:</b> They do not learn faster than specialists. They learn differently. They constantly map new knowledge onto existing knowledge from other domains. This mapping — not raw study hours — is what produces the explosive compound returns that make polymaths appear superhuman. You are not trying to be smart. You are trying to be connected.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================
       TAB 1 — MATHEMATICS
       ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel" id="p1">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-math">Foundations to Frontiers</div>
      <div class="track-title">Mathematics — the universal language of reality</div>
      <div class="track-sub">Mathematics is not a domain alongside others — it is the substrate underneath all of them. Physics is applied mathematics. Computer science is discrete mathematics. ML is optimization theory. Every hour invested in mathematical depth pays compound interest across every other domain in this program. This track takes you from foundational rigor through to the frontiers of modern mathematics.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm4">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm4')">
        <div class="step-num num-math">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Set Theory & Mathematical Logic</div><div class="step-meta">The foundation of all mathematical reasoning</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm4">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm4">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Naive Set Theory" by Paul Halmos</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The classic introduction to set theory. Short (~100 pages), beautifully written, and rigorous enough to build everything else on top of. Covers: sets, relations, functions, ordinals, cardinals, axiom of choice, Zorn's lemma. This tiny book gives you the language that all of mathematics speaks. Read it with pen and paper — prove every theorem yourself before reading Halmos's proof.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Start here</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"How to Prove It" by Daniel Velleman</div>
              <div class="res-desc">If you have not done proof-based mathematics before, read this first. Teaches proof techniques — direct proof, contradiction, induction, contrapositive — through set theory and logic. Every proof technique you learn here is used in every subsequent mathematics course. This is the bridge from computational math to real math.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">If new to proofs</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-510-introduction-to-mathematical-logic-and-set-theory-fall-2024/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT OCW — Introduction to Mathematical Logic <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free MIT course covering propositional logic, first-order logic, completeness, compactness, and undecidability. Pair with Halmos for the set theory side and you have a complete logical foundation.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Why this matters for everything else:</b> Set theory gives you the vocabulary (sets, functions, relations). Logic gives you the grammar (proof, implication, quantification). Without these two, every subsequent math course is a house built on sand. Spend 3-4 weeks here before moving forward. You cannot rush foundations.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm5">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm5')">
        <div class="step-num num-math">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Real Analysis</div><div class="step-meta">Rigorous calculus — the backbone of continuous mathematics</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm5">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm5">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Principles of Mathematical Analysis" by Walter Rudin (Baby Rudin)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The gold standard textbook for rigorous real analysis. Chapters 1-7 cover: the real number system, basic topology, sequences and series, continuity, differentiation, Riemann-Stieltjes integration, and sequences/series of functions. Rudin is famously terse — every line matters, every exercise teaches. This book will teach you to think like a mathematician. Do not skip exercises. Work problems daily.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">The standard</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Calculus" by Michael Spivak</div>
              <div class="res-desc">If Rudin is too dense to start, begin with Spivak. It covers the same material as Baby Rudin but with more motivation and gentler pacing. Spivak's exercises are legendary — they are where the real learning happens. Many professional mathematicians started here. Use Spivak as your on-ramp to Rudin, not a replacement for it.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Gentler on-ramp</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-100a-real-analysis-fall-2020/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 18.100A — Real Analysis (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free MIT course with full lecture notes and problem sets. Professor Casey Rodriguez teaches with exceptional clarity. Use alongside Rudin — watch lectures for intuition, then read Rudin for rigor, then solve the MIT problem sets for practice.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLUl4u3cNGP61O7HkcF7UImpM0cR_L2gSw" target="_blank" class="res-link">Francis Su — Real Analysis Lectures (Harvey Mudd, YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free lecture series that follows Baby Rudin chapter by chapter. Su is one of the most beloved math educators alive. His lectures add the warmth and intuition that Rudin's text intentionally omits. Watch each lecture before tackling the corresponding Rudin chapter.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>Why real analysis matters beyond math:</b> Real analysis gives you the rigorous language of limits, convergence, and continuity that underpins all of: neural network convergence proofs, statistical consistency, optimization theory, signal processing, and quantum mechanics. When you understand epsilon-delta arguments at a gut level, the theoretical ML and physics literature becomes readable instead of opaque.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm6">
