            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-701-algebra-i-fall-2010/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 18.701 — Algebra I (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free MIT algebra course using Artin's textbook. Lecture notes and problem sets available. Covers groups through representation theory. Pair directly with Artin — same order, same notation.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLelIK3uylPMGzHBuR3hLMHrYfMqWWsmx5" target="_blank" class="res-link">Benedict Gross — Abstract Algebra (Harvard, YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Full Harvard undergraduate algebra course, freely available. Gross is an exceptional lecturer who connects algebra to number theory, geometry, and physics throughout. His treatment of group actions and Sylow theorems is particularly illuminating.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Cross-domain connection:</b> Group theory is the mathematics of symmetry. In physics, Noether's theorem says every symmetry of nature corresponds to a conservation law — energy conservation comes from time-translation symmetry, momentum conservation from spatial symmetry. In CS, algebraic structures appear in type theory, cryptography (finite fields, elliptic curves), and error-correcting codes. When you learn groups, you are learning the skeleton key to physics and CS simultaneously.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm7">
