  <div class="panel" id="p2">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-dsa">Classical to Quantum to Cosmological</div>
      <div class="track-title">Physics — understanding reality from first principles</div>
      <div class="track-sub">Physics is the original polymath discipline — it demands mathematics, computation, and engineering simultaneously. The sequence below takes you from classical mechanics through to the frontiers of theoretical physics. The Feynman Lectures on Physics serve as your backbone throughout: a three-volume masterpiece that connects every topic with unmatched clarity and physical intuition.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm10">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm10')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Classical Mechanics</div><div class="step-meta">Goldstein / Feynman Vol. 1 / Lagrangian & Hamiltonian formalism</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm10">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm10">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.feynmanlectures.caltech.edu/" target="_blank" class="res-link">The Feynman Lectures on Physics — Volume I (free online) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Freely available at feynmanlectures.caltech.edu. Start here. Feynman's lectures cover mechanics, thermodynamics, waves, and optics with unparalleled physical intuition. Feynman teaches you to THINK about physics, not just calculate. Read Chapters 1-15 for mechanics, then Chapters 39-52 for thermodynamics and statistical mechanics. Every physicist alive was shaped by these lectures.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">The backbone</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Classical Mechanics" by Herbert Goldstein, Poole & Safko</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The standard graduate text. Covers Lagrangian mechanics, Hamiltonian mechanics, canonical transformations, Hamilton-Jacobi theory, rigid body dynamics, and classical chaos. The Lagrangian and Hamiltonian formulations are not just elegant math — they are the direct precursors to quantum mechanics and quantum field theory. You cannot do theoretical physics without this language.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Graduate standard</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-01sc-classical-mechanics-fall-2016/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 8.01 Classical Mechanics (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Full MIT mechanics course with video lectures, problem sets, and exams. Covers Newtonian mechanics, rotational dynamics, and central forces. Excellent preparation before tackling Goldstein's Lagrangian formalism.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL47F408D36D4CF129" target="_blank" class="res-link">Leonard Susskind — Classical Mechanics (Stanford, YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Stanford's "Theoretical Minimum" lecture series. Susskind teaches the minimum you need to do real physics — Lagrangians, Hamiltonians, phase space, Poisson brackets. Free on YouTube. More mathematically rigorous than typical undergraduate courses but deeply motivated by physical intuition.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Connection to robotics:</b> Lagrangian mechanics is exactly how we derive equations of motion for robots. Every joint of a robotic arm is a generalized coordinate in a Lagrangian. When you learn Goldstein Chapter 1-2, you are simultaneously learning robot dynamics. This is the polymath advantage: one effort, two domains mastered.</div>
