            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-02-physics-ii-electricity-and-magnetism-spring-2019/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 8.02 — Electricity and Magnetism (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free MIT course with full video lectures by Walter Lewin (legendary demonstrations) or more recent versions. Use for visualization alongside Griffiths text. The demonstrations alone — from Van de Graaff generators to electromagnetic induction — make abstract concepts unforgettable.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@PBSSpaceTime" target="_blank" class="res-link">PBS Space Time — Electromagnetism playlist (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">PBS Space Time covers electromagnetic phenomena with beautiful animations and deep conceptual clarity. Their episodes on Maxwell's equations, light, and electromagnetic radiation connect E&M to relativity and quantum mechanics in ways that textbooks often miss.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>Why E&M matters beyond physics:</b> Maxwell's equations unify electricity, magnetism, and light. Understanding them deeply connects to: signal processing (electromagnetic waves), antenna design and wireless engineering, photonics, and the gauge symmetry concepts that underpin all of modern particle physics. Griffiths Chapter 7-9 on electromagnetic waves is also the theoretical foundation for all wireless communication.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm12">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm12')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Quantum Mechanics</div><div class="step-meta">Sakurai / Griffiths QM / the heart of modern physics</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm12">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm12">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Introduction to Quantum Mechanics" by Griffiths & Schroeter</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Start here. Griffiths brings the same clarity to QM that he brings to E&M. Covers: Schrodinger equation, formalism (Hilbert space), angular momentum, spin, identical particles, perturbation theory, scattering. The wave-function-first approach builds intuition before formalism. Do every problem.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Start here</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Modern Quantum Mechanics" by J.J. Sakurai & Jim Napolitano</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The graduate standard. Sakurai starts with Stern-Gerlach experiments and builds quantum mechanics from the formalism of kets, bras, and operators — the opposite of Griffiths' approach. Covers: angular momentum, symmetry, perturbation theory, scattering, relativistic QM. After Griffiths gives you intuition, Sakurai gives you the operator-algebraic framework that connects directly to QFT.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Graduate standard</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-04-quantum-physics-i-spring-2016/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 8.04 / 8.05 — Quantum Physics I & II (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Full MIT quantum mechanics sequence with video lectures by Barton Zwiebach. Excellent visual demonstrations and step-by-step problem solving. 8.04 covers foundations, 8.05 covers the formalism and applications. Free problem sets and exams.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@LookingGlassUniverse" target="_blank" class="res-link">Looking Glass Universe — Quantum Mechanics (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Mithuna Yoganathan's channel provides some of the clearest conceptual explanations of quantum mechanics on YouTube. Her series on quantum entanglement, measurement, and the path integral formulation complement textbook study beautifully.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>The math prerequisite:</b> You need linear algebra (operators on Hilbert spaces), complex analysis (wave functions are complex-valued), and differential equations. If your functional analysis from the math track is solid, Sakurai will feel natural. If not, start with Griffiths QM and return to Sakurai after more mathematical preparation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm13">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm13')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">4</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Statistical Mechanics, General Relativity & QFT</div><div class="step-meta">The advanced frontier of physics</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm13">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm13">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Statistical Mechanics" by R.K. Pathria & Paul Beale</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The comprehensive graduate text. Covers: ensembles, quantum statistics, Bose-Einstein and Fermi-Dirac systems, phase transitions, fluctuations, and non-equilibrium stat mech. Statistical mechanics is where physics meets information theory and probability — the connection to ML is direct (Boltzmann machines, energy-based models, simulated annealing all come from stat mech concepts).</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Graduate standard</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Spacetime and Geometry" by Sean Carroll</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The most readable introduction to general relativity. Carroll develops differential geometry and tensor calculus as needed, then derives Einstein's field equations, Schwarzschild solution, gravitational waves, and cosmology. Clear, modern, and well-motivated. Requires: multivariable calculus, linear algebra, and ideally some differential geometry (do Carmo).</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Best intro to GR</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"An Introduction to Quantum Field Theory" by Peskin & Schroeder</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The standard QFT textbook. Covers: canonical quantization, Feynman diagrams, QED, renormalization, non-abelian gauge theories, and the Standard Model. This is the summit of the physics track — the framework that unifies quantum mechanics and special relativity. Extremely demanding. Prerequisites: Sakurai-level QM, classical mechanics (Lagrangian/Hamiltonian), and solid real/complex analysis.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag book">Advanced</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@pbsspacetime" target="_blank" class="res-link">PBS Space Time (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The best science communication channel for theoretical physics. Episodes on general relativity, quantum field theory, string theory, and cosmology are produced at a level that is deep enough to supplement graduate study while remaining visually engaging. Watch their GR and QFT series alongside your textbook study.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@veaborea" target="_blank" class="res-link">Veritasium — Physics explainers (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Derek Muller's channel provides deeply researched, high-production-value videos on physics topics from turbulence to quantum entanglement. His videos on general relativity and the nature of gravity are excellent supplementary viewing.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>String theory primer:</b> After QFT, for string theory basics start with Barton Zwiebach's "A First Course in String Theory" — it is designed to be accessible with just QM and special relativity. Polchinski's two volumes are the graduate standard but require solid QFT. String theory is where physics, differential geometry, algebraic geometry, and topology all converge — the ultimate polymath subject.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================
       TAB 3 — COMPUTER SCIENCE
       ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel" id="p3">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-ml">Theory to Systems</div>
      <div class="track-title">Computer Science — the science of computation and abstraction</div>
      <div class="track-sub">Computer science is not programming. It is the mathematical study of computation, information, and abstraction. This track follows the intellectual spine of CS from the theory of what is computable, through algorithms, programming languages, compilers, operating systems, and up to formal verification — the frontier where CS meets mathematical logic.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm14">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm14')">
        <div class="step-num num-ml">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Theory of Computation</div><div class="step-meta">Sipser / What is computable and what is not</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm14">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm14">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Introduction to the Theory of Computation" by Michael Sipser</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The clearest book ever written on automata theory, computability, and complexity. Covers: finite automata, regular languages, context-free grammars, Turing machines, decidability, reducibility, time complexity (P vs NP), space complexity. Sipser's proofs are models of clarity. Every CS student should work through this book cover to cover. The chapter on P vs NP will change how you think about the limits of computation forever.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">The standard</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-404j-theory-of-computation-fall-2020/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 18.404J — Theory of Computation (OCW, taught by Sipser) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Sipser himself teaching his own book at MIT. Free video lectures, problem sets, and exams. There is no better way to learn this material than from the person who wrote the definitive text. His lectures add intuition and motivation that even his excellent book sometimes omits.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Taught by the author</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Why this matters for a polymath:</b> Theory of computation teaches you the absolute limits of what machines can do. The halting problem proves some things are fundamentally uncomputable. P vs NP asks whether verification is easier than discovery — a question with implications for cryptography, optimization, AI, and even the philosophy of mathematics. This is where CS becomes philosophy.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm15">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm15')">
        <div class="step-num num-ml">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Algorithms & Data Structures</div><div class="step-meta">CLRS / the engineering of efficiency</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm15">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm15">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Introduction to Algorithms" by Cormen, Leiserson, Rivest, Stein (CLRS)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The bible of algorithms. Covers: sorting, data structures (heaps, BSTs, hash tables, red-black trees), graph algorithms (BFS, DFS, shortest paths, MSTs), dynamic programming, greedy algorithms, amortized analysis, NP-completeness, approximation algorithms. 1300+ pages of the most rigorous algorithm analysis available. Use as a reference throughout your career — nobody reads it linearly.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">The bible</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 6.006 — Introduction to Algorithms (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free MIT algorithms course with full video lectures by Erik Demaine and Jason Ku. Covers the core CLRS chapters with excellent visualizations and worked examples. Follow with MIT 6.046 (Design and Analysis of Algorithms) for advanced topics.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLXFMmlk03Dt7Q0xr1PIAriY5623cKiH7V" target="_blank" class="res-link">Stanford Algorithms — Tim Roughgarden (YouTube/Coursera) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Roughgarden's algorithm courses are famously well-taught. Available on YouTube and as a Coursera specialization (audit free). His explanations of divide-and-conquer, graph algorithms, and greedy algorithms are particularly strong. Excellent complement to CLRS.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm16">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm16')">
        <div class="step-num num-ml">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Programming Languages & Compilers</div><div class="step-meta">SICP / Dragon Book / the soul of software</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm16">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm16">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://mitp-content-server.mit.edu/books/content/sectbyfn/books_pres_0/6515/sicp.zip/full-text/book/book.html" target="_blank" class="res-link">"Structure and Interpretation of Computer Programs" (SICP) — free online <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The most intellectually transformative CS textbook ever written. Abelson and Sussman teach you to think about computation itself — abstraction, recursion, interpretation, metalinguistic abstraction. You build a Scheme interpreter inside Scheme. By the end, you understand what a programming language IS at a fundamental level. Free online. MIT used this as their intro CS course for decades.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">Life-changing</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Compilers: Principles, Techniques, and Tools" (Dragon Book) by Aho, Lam, Sethi, Ullman</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The definitive compiler textbook. Covers: lexical analysis, parsing (LL, LR, LALR), syntax-directed translation, intermediate representations, code generation, optimization. Building a compiler is one of the most educational projects in all of CS — it touches automata theory, data structures, algorithms, and architecture simultaneously. The Dragon Book is dense but comprehensive.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Classic</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PL_2VhOvlMk4UHo_GcoswPVnRLa1Wn2LMu" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 6.001 SICP Lectures — Abelson & Sussman (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The original 1986 MIT SICP lectures. Abelson and Sussman teaching their own masterpiece. Despite being recorded decades ago, these lectures remain unmatched for depth and clarity. Watch them alongside reading the book — the authors add stories, motivations, and connections that the text alone cannot convey.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://craftinginterpreters.com/" target="_blank" class="res-link">"Crafting Interpreters" by Robert Nystrom (free online) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free at craftinginterpreters.com. A beautifully written, practical guide to building two complete interpreters — one in Java (tree-walk), one in C (bytecode VM). More accessible than the Dragon Book for a first compiler project. Do this before or alongside the Dragon Book.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">Build one</span><span class="rtag tool">Project</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>The SICP effect:</b> SICP will change how you think about all software. After working through it, you will see every programming language as a set of design choices, every framework as a thin abstraction, and every "paradigm war" (OOP vs FP) as a false dichotomy. SICP graduates think at a level of abstraction above most programmers. That level of abstraction is where polymath insights live.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm17">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm17')">
        <div class="step-num num-ml">4</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Operating Systems, Networks, Databases & Cryptography</div><div class="step-meta">Systems infrastructure + formal verification</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm17">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm17">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~remzi/OSTEP/" target="_blank" class="res-link">"Operating Systems: Three Easy Pieces" (OSTEP) — free online <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free at ostep.org. The best OS textbook available. Covers: virtualization (processes, memory), concurrency (threads, locks, semaphores, condition variables), persistence (file systems, I/O). Written with humor and exceptional clarity. The concurrency chapters alone are worth reading for anyone writing multithreaded code.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag must">Best OS text</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Computer Networking: A Top-Down Approach" by Kurose & Ross</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The standard networking textbook. Starts from the application layer (HTTP, DNS, SMTP) and works down to the link layer. Clear, well-illustrated, with Wireshark labs that let you see real network traffic. Understanding networking is essential for distributed systems, cloud computing, and security engineering.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Standard text</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Designing Data-Intensive Applications" by Martin Kleppmann (DDIA)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The modern bible for databases and distributed data systems. Covers: data models, storage engines, replication, partitioning, transactions, consistency, batch/stream processing. Kleppmann explains the trade-offs behind every database design choice. This book bridges CS theory and practical distributed systems engineering.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Modern classic</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Introduction to Modern Cryptography" by Katz & Lindell</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Rigorous, proof-based introduction to cryptography. Covers: symmetric encryption, public-key encryption, digital signatures, hash functions, zero-knowledge proofs. Cryptography is where number theory, algebra, and CS theory converge. This book treats crypto as a mathematical discipline, not a cookbook of protocols.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Math meets CS</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Software Foundations" — online textbook (free, Coq-based)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free at softwarefoundations.cis.upenn.edu. Interactive textbook teaching formal verification using the Coq proof assistant. You learn to write mathematical proofs that are machine-checked — programs that are provably correct. This is the frontier of CS where programming meets mathematical logic. Connects directly to type theory, category theory, and the foundations of mathematics.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free online</span><span class="rtag tool">Interactive</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Recommended study order:</b> OSTEP first (you need to understand processes and memory to understand anything else). Then networking (Kurose). Then databases (DDIA). Then cryptography (Katz & Lindell — needs number theory from the math track). Formal verification last — it requires mathematical maturity from all previous tracks combined.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================
       TAB 4 — ENGINEERING & ROBOTICS
       ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel" id="p4">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-java">Signals to Autonomous Systems</div>
      <div class="track-title">Engineering & Robotics — where math meets the physical world</div>
      <div class="track-sub">This track takes you from the mathematical foundations of engineering (control theory, signal processing) through electronics and embedded systems to the frontier of autonomous robotics. Every topic here is a direct application of the mathematics and physics from the earlier tabs. This is where abstraction becomes reality.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm18">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm18')">
        <div class="step-num num-java">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Control Systems</div><div class="step-meta">Feedback, stability, and the mathematics of regulation</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm18">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm18">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Feedback Control of Dynamic Systems" by Franklin, Powell & Emami-Naeini</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The standard undergraduate control systems text. Covers: modeling physical systems, feedback fundamentals, root locus, frequency response (Bode, Nyquist), state-space design, digital control. Control theory is the mathematics of making systems behave — it appears in robotics, aerospace, process engineering, and even in ML (gradient descent IS a control system where the loss is the error signal).</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Standard text</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLUMWjy5jgHK1NC52DXXrriwihVrYZKqjk" target="_blank" class="res-link">Brian Douglas — Control Systems Lectures (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The best free video series on control systems. Brian Douglas explains transfer functions, PID control, Bode plots, and state-space methods with clarity that most professors cannot match. His MATLAB/Simulink demonstrations show theory becoming working controllers. Essential companion to any textbook.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Best video series</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-003-signals-and-systems-fall-2011/" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 6.003 — Signals and Systems (OCW) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free MIT course covering the prerequisite mathematics for control systems: Fourier series, Fourier transform, Laplace transform, Z-transform, LTI systems. These transforms are the mathematical backbone of signal processing AND control theory. Master them once, use them in both fields.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">MIT OCW</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Project idea:</b> Build a PID controller for a simple system — balancing an inverted pendulum in simulation (Python + scipy). This single project combines: differential equations, linear algebra (state-space representation), control theory, and Python programming. Simulate in Python, then implement on an Arduino for the real-world version.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm19">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm19')">
        <div class="step-num num-java">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Signal Processing & Electronics</div><div class="step-meta">From Fourier to circuits to embedded systems</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm19">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm19">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Discrete-Time Signal Processing" by Oppenheim & Schafer</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The definitive DSP textbook. Covers: discrete-time signals, Z-transform, DFT/FFT, filter design, multirate signal processing. DSP is where complex analysis, linear algebra, and engineering converge. The FFT alone — the most important algorithm of the 20th century — connects to everything from audio processing to quantum simulation.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">The standard</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"The Art of Electronics" by Horowitz & Hill</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The legendary electronics book, now in its 3rd edition. Covers: resistors, capacitors, transistors, op-amps, digital logic, microcontrollers. Written for practitioners, not theorists — Horowitz and Hill teach by building circuits, not solving equations. If you want to understand what happens inside the hardware that runs your software, this is the book.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Legendary</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@BenEater" target="_blank" class="res-link">Ben Eater — Building a Computer from Scratch (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Ben Eater builds an 8-bit computer on breadboards from individual logic gates. This is the single best resource for understanding how hardware actually works — from NAND gates to a functioning CPU with RAM and I/O. His 6502 computer project is equally excellent. Watch every video if you want to understand embedded systems from the transistor up.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Watch all</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>Embedded systems path:</b> After electronics fundamentals, study embedded C programming on ARM microcontrollers (STM32). Resources: "Making Embedded Systems" by Elecia White, and the STM32 CubeIDE tutorials. Embedded is where software meets hardware — the bridge between your CS knowledge and physical robotics systems.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm20">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm20')">
        <div class="step-num num-java">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Robot Kinematics, Computer Vision & Autonomous Systems</div><div class="step-meta">ROS2 / perception / navigation / FPGA design</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm20">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm20">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Modern Robotics: Mechanics, Planning, and Control" by Lynch & Park (free PDF)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free PDF and Coursera specialization. Covers: rigid body motions, forward/inverse kinematics, velocity kinematics, dynamics, motion planning, robot control. Uses Lie group theory (screw theory) for kinematics — more elegant and general than Denavit-Hartenberg. The math connects directly to differential geometry from the math track.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free PDF + Coursera</span><span class="rtag must">Primary text</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Multiple View Geometry in Computer Vision" by Hartley & Zisserman</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The definitive text on geometric computer vision — epipolar geometry, fundamental matrix, homography, 3D reconstruction, camera calibration. This is the mathematical backbone of how robots see the world. Heavy on projective geometry and linear algebra. Essential for any work in visual SLAM, autonomous driving, or AR/VR.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Vision bible</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://docs.ros.org/en/humble/Tutorials.html" target="_blank" class="res-link">ROS2 Humble — Official Tutorials <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">ROS2 (Robot Operating System 2) is the standard framework for building robot software. The official tutorials take you from installation through nodes, topics, services, actions, and launch files. After completing tutorials, build a simulated robot in Gazebo that navigates autonomously using Nav2. ROS2 is the practical glue that connects all robotics theory into working systems.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Industry standard</span><span class="rtag tool">Framework</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://cs231n.stanford.edu/" target="_blank" class="res-link">Stanford CS231n — Convolutional Neural Networks for Visual Recognition <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Free course notes, assignments, and lecture videos. Covers CNNs, object detection, segmentation, generative models — the deep learning side of computer vision for robotics. After Hartley & Zisserman give you the geometric foundations, CS231n gives you the neural network perception stack that modern robots use.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag vid">Stanford</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Digital Design and Computer Architecture" by Harris & Harris (for FPGA)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">For FPGA design, start here. Covers digital logic, processor architecture, and hardware description languages (SystemVerilog). FPGAs let you design custom hardware — essential for high-performance robotics, signal processing, and neural network accelerators. Follow with Xilinx/AMD or Intel FPGA tutorials for practical implementation.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span><span class="rtag tool">FPGA</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>The capstone robotics project:</b> Build a ROS2-based robot (simulated in Gazebo or on real hardware like TurtleBot) that uses a camera for perception (YOLO/OpenCV), SLAM for mapping, and Nav2 for autonomous navigation. This single project integrates: control systems, kinematics, computer vision, embedded systems, and software engineering. It is a polymath project by definition.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================================================
       TAB 5 — SOFTWARE ENGINEERING
       ============================================================ -->
  <div class="panel" id="p5">
    <div class="track-header">
      <div class="track-badge badge-dsa">Architecture to Leadership</div>
      <div class="track-title">Software Engineering — building systems that endure</div>
      <div class="track-sub">Software engineering is distinct from computer science the way civil engineering is distinct from physics. CS asks "what is computable?" Software engineering asks "how do we build large, correct, maintainable, performant systems with teams of humans?" This track covers the craft of building real software at scale — from architecture patterns through distributed systems to technical leadership.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm21">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm21')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">1</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Architecture Patterns & Design</div><div class="step-meta">How to structure systems that survive</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm21">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm21">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Designing Data-Intensive Applications" by Martin Kleppmann (DDIA)</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Listed again here because it bridges CS and software engineering. DDIA explains the fundamental trade-offs in every architectural decision: consistency vs availability, latency vs throughput, simplicity vs flexibility. Read cover to cover. This is the single most important book for anyone building backend systems.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Read cover to cover</span><span class="rtag book">Textbook</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"Clean Architecture" by Robert C. Martin</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Principles of software architecture: dependency inversion, separation of concerns, ports and adapters, the clean architecture pattern. Martin's framework for thinking about boundaries between components applies to any system in any language. Read alongside DDIA — Martin gives you the principles, Kleppmann gives you the implementations.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Principles</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"A Philosophy of Software Design" by John Ousterhout</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Short, opinionated, and brilliant. Ousterhout (creator of Tcl) argues that the central problem of software design is managing complexity. His concepts of "deep modules" and "information hiding" will change how you design every API and interface. Read in a weekend, apply for a career.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Quick essential read</span><span class="rtag book">Book</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm22">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm22')">
        <div class="step-num num-dsa">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Distributed Systems & Cloud Native</div><div class="step-meta">Building at scale</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm22">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm22">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/playlist?list=PLrw6a1wE39_tb2fErI4-WkMbsvGQk9_UB" target="_blank" class="res-link">MIT 6.824 — Distributed Systems (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Robert Morris's MIT distributed systems course. Free lectures on YouTube, with labs where you build Raft consensus, MapReduce, and a fault-tolerant key-value store in Go. This is widely considered the best distributed systems course available anywhere. The labs alone will teach you more about real distributed systems than any amount of reading.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Best DS course</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
