      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm25">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">What are PINNs?</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Physics-Informed Neural Networks encode known physical laws (differential equations, conservation laws, symmetries) directly into the loss function of a neural network. Instead of learning physics purely from data, the network is constrained to obey physical laws. This reduces data requirements dramatically, produces physically plausible predictions, and enables scientific discovery. PINNs are one of the hottest research areas in scientific computing.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1711.10561" target="_blank" class="res-link">Raissi, Perdikaris, Karniadakis — "Physics Informed Deep Learning" (arXiv) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">The foundational PINN paper. Read after you have solid DL foundations and understand PDEs from the physics track. The key insight: add a PDE residual term to the standard neural network loss function. The network then learns to satisfy the PDE while fitting the data. This paper launched an entire research field.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Foundational paper</span><span class="rtag book">Research</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><polygon points="10 8 16 12 10 16 10 8"></polygon></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://www.youtube.com/@Eigensteve" target="_blank" class="res-link">Steve Brunton — Data-Driven Science (YouTube) <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">Steve Brunton's channel at University of Washington covers the intersection of data science, dynamical systems, and scientific computing. His playlists on PINNs, sparse identification of nonlinear dynamics (SINDy), and dynamic mode decomposition are the best free resources at the physics-ML intersection. His book "Data-Driven Science and Engineering" is also excellent.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Best physics-ML channel</span><span class="rtag vid">YouTube</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>Why PINNs need a polymath:</b> To build effective PINNs, you need: (1) Deep learning expertise to design the network architecture. (2) Physics knowledge to write the correct PDE constraints. (3) Numerical analysis to understand discretization and convergence. (4) Domain expertise to interpret results. No single-domain expert has all four. This is the prototypical polymath research problem.</div>
