          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>The rule of cross-domain projects:</b> Every project you build from this point forward should touch at least 3 domains. Single-domain projects prove competence. Cross-domain projects prove synthesis. Synthesis is what makes a polymath — and what makes you irreplaceable.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm29">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm29')">
        <div class="step-num num-math">2</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Research at Intersections</div><div class="step-meta">Finding and publishing interdisciplinary work</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm29">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm29">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">How to find research problems at intersections</div>
              <div class="res-desc">The richest research problems live at boundaries between fields. To find them: (1) Read papers from two different fields in the same week and ask "what technique from field A would solve a problem in field B?" (2) Attend talks and conferences outside your primary domain. (3) Follow researchers who work at intersections — they model the path. (4) When you learn a new technique, immediately ask "where else could this apply?" The best intersection problems are ones where neither field alone has the tools to solve them, but both together do.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name"><a href="https://arxiv.org/" target="_blank" class="res-link">arXiv — free research paper repository <span class="res-link-arrow">&nearr;</span></a></div>
              <div class="res-desc">All cutting-edge research in physics, mathematics, CS, and ML is posted on arXiv for free. Build a daily habit: read one abstract per day from a field adjacent to your main focus. Use Semantic Scholar (semanticscholar.org) or Connected Papers (connectedpapers.com) to find related work across field boundaries. Subscribe to arXiv categories in at least two domains.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag free">Free</span><span class="rtag must">Daily habit</span><span class="rtag tool">Research</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">Publishing venues for interdisciplinary work</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Interdisciplinary journals and conferences: Nature, Science, PNAS (broad science). NeurIPS, ICML, ICLR (ML with growing physics/science tracks). Journal of Machine Learning Research (JMLR, free and open access). Physical Review X (physics + computation). AAAI (AI broadly). Workshop papers at major conferences are excellent entry points — many NeurIPS/ICML workshops specifically seek cross-domain work. Start with workshop papers, build toward main conference submissions.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="insight"><b>The publication strategy for a polymath:</b> Do not try to publish in the top venue of each field. Instead, find venues that VALUE cross-domain work. Workshop papers at NeurIPS/ICML that combine physics+ML are easier to get accepted than pure ML papers at the same venues, because you face less competition and reviewers value novelty of approach. Your polymath background IS your competitive advantage in publishing.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step" id="pm30">
      <div class="step-head" onclick="tog('pm30')">
        <div class="step-num num-math">3</div>
        <div class="step-info"><div class="step-title">Building a Polymath Identity</div><div class="step-meta">How to present and compound your cross-domain expertise</div></div>
        <div class="step-arrow" id="a-pm30">&#9660;</div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-body" id="b-pm30">
        <div class="res-list">
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">The portfolio architecture</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Your public presence should make your cross-domain expertise visible and legible. GitHub: organized by domain with cross-references between repos. Personal website/blog: write posts that explicitly connect two domains ("What quantum mechanics taught me about neural network optimization"). LinkedIn: headline that names your intersection ("AI + Physics + Robotics"). Each piece of public work should demonstrate synthesis, not just competence in one area.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">The teaching advantage</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Teach what you learn. Write blog posts, make YouTube videos, give talks at meetups. Teaching forces the Feynman Technique on yourself — you cannot explain what you do not deeply understand. But more importantly, teaching builds your public identity. A person who writes a blog post titled "Category Theory for Machine Learning Engineers" is immediately identified as someone who bridges fields. That identity compounds — people start bringing you problems that live at intersections.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon">&#9670;</div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">The network effect</div>
              <div class="res-desc">Polymaths need networks in multiple communities. Join: local math meetups, ML reading groups, robotics clubs, physics colloquia, open source projects in different domains. Online: follow researchers at intersections on Twitter/X, join Discord servers for different domains (ML Collective, Physics Forums, Robotics Worldwide). Each community gives you problems, collaborators, and perspectives that your other communities lack. Your cross-community position IS your unfair advantage.</div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="res-item">
            <div class="res-icon"><svg class="res-svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 19.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 17H20"></path><path d="M6.5 2H20v20H6.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-15A2.5 2.5 0 0 1 6.5 2z"></path></svg></div>
            <div class="res-content">
              <div class="res-name">"The Polymath Playbook" — essential reading list for the polymath path</div>
              <div class="res-desc">"Range" by David Epstein (why generalists win). "Ultralearning" by Scott Young (how to learn anything fast). "Poor Charlie's Almanack" by Charlie Munger (latticework of mental models). "Surely You're Joking, Mr. Feynman!" by Richard Feynman (the polymath life). "The Art of Doing Science and Engineering" by Richard Hamming (how to do great work). "Godel, Escher, Bach" by Douglas Hofstadter (the ultimate cross-domain book — mathematics, art, music, AI, consciousness). Read one per quarter.</div>
              <div class="res-tags"><span class="rtag must">Essential reading</span><span class="rtag book">Books</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="do-box"><b>Your 48-month milestone check:</b> By the end of this program, you should be able to: (1) Read a research paper from any domain in this program and understand the core argument. (2) Build a project that touches 3+ domains. (3) Explain a concept from one domain using the language of another. (4) Identify research problems at intersections that specialists miss. (5) Have a public body of work that demonstrates synthesis, not just collection. If you can do all five, you are a polymath. Not because you know everything — but because you can connect anything.</div>
